{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HeckeStableScalarCascade","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HeckeStableScalarCascade","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HeckeJucysMurphyElements"],"declarations":23,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.existsNonscalar_heckeGenerators","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.existsNonscalar_heckeGenerators","kind":"theorem","line":303,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L303-L313","doc":"**The exclusion is sharp.**  There *is* a `HeckeGenerators` instance on `2×2` matrices whose\ngenerators are not scalar — so `H_∞(q)` does have multi-dimensional representations, and what\n§3 excludes is precisely the **irreducible** ones.\n\nAny reading of `stdTwo_no_stable_extension` as \"no `dim > 1` instance exists\" is refuted by this. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwo_not_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwo_not_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":290,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L290-L301","doc":"It is **not** a scalar matrix, provided `q² ≠ −1` (i.e. `q ≠ −q⁻¹`, so the two Hecke\neigenvalues are distinct). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwo_hecke","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwo_hecke","kind":"theorem","line":281,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L281-L288","doc":"`diag(q, −q⁻¹)` satisfies the Hecke relation, in the class's `algebraMap` vocabulary. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwoInv_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwoInv_mul","kind":"theorem","line":276,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L276-L279","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwo_mul_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwo_mul_inv","kind":"theorem","line":271,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L271-L274","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwoInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwoInv","kind":"def","line":268,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L268-L269","doc":"Its inverse `diag(q⁻¹, −q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.diagTwo","kind":"def","line":264,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L264-L266","doc":"The reducible 2-dimensional generator `diag(q, −q⁻¹)`: the two Hecke eigenvalues placed on\nthe diagonal, so each coordinate line is invariant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.constGenerators","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.constGenerators","kind":"def","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L244-L258","doc":"**Constant generators.**  Any Hecke-quadratic unit `X` gives a `HeckeGenerators` with\n`σᵢ = X` for every `i`.\n\nBraid and far-commutation are `rfl` once all generators coincide, so the only real content is the\nHecke relation.  This is the general form of the counterexample that refutes reading the cascade\nas a statement about dimension. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_no_stable_extension","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_no_stable_extension","kind":"theorem","line":225,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L225-L238","doc":"**The obstruction, concretely.**  There is no `HeckeGenerators` structure on `2×2` matrices\nwhose first two generators are the `(2,1)` seminormal pair.\n\nSo the stable presentation cannot host `stdRep3`, and the `dim > 1` witness for `IsJMWeight`\ncannot be obtained by instantiating the class as it stands (bean `qou-t1kv`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo1_not_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo1_not_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L218-L223","doc":"`σ₁` of the explicit pair is **not** a scalar: its `(1,0)` entry is `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_centralizer_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_centralizer_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":203,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L203-L216","doc":"**Schur for the explicit pair.**  Anything commuting with both `σ₀` and `σ₁` is scalar. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_hecke1_algebraMap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_hecke1_algebraMap","kind":"theorem","line":196,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L196-L201","doc":"`σ₁`'s Hecke relation in the class's own vocabulary. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_hecke0_algebraMap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_hecke0_algebraMap","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L188-L194","doc":"`σ₀`'s Hecke relation in the **class's own vocabulary** (`algebraMap`, not `•`), so that the\nnon-vacuity claim is literally the relation `HeckeGenerators.hecke` demands. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_braid","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L179-L186","doc":"`σ₀` and `σ₁` satisfy the braid relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_hecke1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_hecke1","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L170-L177","doc":"`σ₁` satisfies the Hecke relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_hecke0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo_hecke0","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L161-L168","doc":"`σ₀` satisfies the Hecke relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo1","kind":"def","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L158-L159","doc":"`σ₁` in Young seminormal form for the `(2,1)` representation of `H₃(q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.stdTwo0","kind":"def","line":155,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L155-L156","doc":"`σ₀` in Young seminormal form for the `(2,1)` representation of `H₃(q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.sigma_scalar_of_schur","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.sigma_scalar_of_schur","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L136-L147","doc":"**The exclusion.**  If the joint centralizer of `σ₀` and `σ₁` consists of scalars — the Schur\ncondition satisfied by any irreducible representation — then `σ₁` is itself a scalar.\n\n`far` places `σ₃` in that centralizer (`0 + 1 < 3` and `1 + 1 < 3`), so `σ₃` is a scalar `s`; the\ncascade of §2 then drags `σ₂` and `σ₁` down to `s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.sigma_eq_of_le_of_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.sigma_eq_of_le_of_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L122-L132","doc":"**Downward propagation.**  A scalar at index `j` forces the same scalar at every `i ≤ j`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.sigma_eq_of_succ_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.sigma_eq_of_succ_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L91-L120","doc":"**Cascade step.**  In `H_∞(q)`, if `σ (i+1)` is the scalar `s` then `σ i` is *the same*\nscalar `s`.\n\nProof: the braid relation at `i` reads `σᵢ · s · σᵢ = s · σᵢ · s`.  Scalars are central, so this\nis `s · σᵢ² = s² · σᵢ`.  Substituting the Hecke relation `σᵢ² = (q − q⁻¹)σᵢ + 1` and collecting\ngives `s·(s − (q − q⁻¹)) · σᵢ = s`, and the bracket is `1` by `scalar_mul_sub_eq_one`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.scalar_mul_sub_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.scalar_mul_sub_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L83-L87","doc":"The scalar in `scalar_hecke_root` is a unit: `s · (s − (q − q⁻¹)) = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.scalar_hecke_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.scalar_hecke_root","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeStableScalarCascade.lean#L71-L81","doc":"If a generator is the scalar `s`, the Hecke relation forces `s² = (q − q⁻¹)s + 1`.\n\n`Nontrivial A` is what makes `algebraMap` from the field `R` injective; without it `A` is the\nzero ring and every relation holds vacuously. ","signatureChunk":0}]}